## 1. Estimand

I recommend **22 independent candidate libraries at R = 12, each scored against the same 20 fresh targets**. The primary estimand is \(p_{12}(\mathcal T)\), the probability that a fresh library passes all ten arm-(i) predicates on the realized target panel \(\mathcal T\). This is an operational measure of four-dimensional recovery at the frozen truth, grid, scenario and objective. The library is the unit of replication.

I propose **80% library reliability** as the decision standard. That is a judgment for the owner to rule on. It allows up to one failed library in five in the underlying draw distribution and has a different meaning from the 80% per-dimension target threshold.

- **Yes** means the study supports \(p_{12}(\mathcal T)>0.80\).
- **No** means the study supports \(p_{12}(\mathcal T)<0.80\). Mixed verdicts show that the draw can flip the result on identical targets. Uniform failure shows that recovery was absent across the draws tested.
- **Can’t tell** means the evidence leaves the 80% standard unresolved, or a shared target predicate prevents the library comparison from answering the question. Mixed verdicts can demonstrate a draw effect even when the reliability standard remains unresolved.

The conditioning on the target panel belongs in every reading. This is synthetic evidence for a later Steven decision. Real-data fitting remains STOPPED. [G41 owner ruling, §§3–5](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-11-20-g41-owner-ruling.md:38)

## 2. Number of libraries and targets

**\(L=22\).** Choose the count by requiring the design to support a positive reading with one observed failure and a one-sided tail of at most 5% at the proposed reliability boundary. Under the independent-library draw model, \(P_{0.8}(K\ge L-1)\) is 5.76% at 21 libraries and 4.80% at 22. So 22 is the smallest count that meets both requirements. Allowing one failure avoids making the positive reading hinge on unanimity. The 80% standard and the 5% evidence threshold are proposed design choices for the owner. Neither comes from the retained outcomes or bootstrap.

This is a demanding confirmation design. For illustration, if conditional reliability were 90%, the positive reading would occur about 34% of the time. At 95%, it would occur about 70% of the time. Those are operating characteristics under hypothetical probabilities. Each extra library buys another draw and costs 300 latent calls and 7,500 report transforms.

**\(N=20\).** Doubling from ten makes each target worth five percentage points in the hit and boundary counts. It reduces the influence of a single unusual target and gives more opportunity to see whether the same targets drive disagreement across libraries. Ten more targets cost ten builds, ten latent calls and ten transforms for the whole campaign, plus scoring against every library and reference batch. Larger panels would further stabilize the fractional counts while giving the all-target viability and no-tie predicates more chances to bind. Their joint operating behavior is unknown. The owner should rule on \(N\), and Steven should receive the fraction-preserving change and its operating-characteristic table.

| Hypothetical per-target success rate | Dimension count met, N=10 | N=20 | Joint count met, N=10 | N=20 |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0.50 | 0.055 | 0.006 | 0.377 | 0.252 |
| 0.60 | 0.167 | 0.051 | 0.633 | 0.596 |
| 0.70 | 0.383 | 0.238 | 0.850 | 0.887 |
| 0.80 | 0.678 | 0.630 | 0.967 | 0.990 |
| 0.90 | 0.930 | 0.957 | 0.998 | 1.000 |

These are single-predicate binomial illustrations for a fixed library under independent target draws. For the boundary count, use the dimension columns with the probability of an interior selection. The composite gate also depends on correlations among predicates and on the all-target rules. More targets cannot replace library draws. [G41 draft, §7.3](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-11-19-g41-confirmatory-design-opus-DRAFT.md:170)

## 3. Independent libraries and shared targets

Generate one fresh panel and share its observed tables byte-for-byte across every library and batch. Each target gets its own process and observation pair. Every library gets twelve fresh pairs, assigned in advance to four ordered batches of three. Freeze an outcome-blind seed-generation procedure and ledger. Check disjointness across process seeds, observation seeds, roles, libraries, reserves and earlier campaigns, and verify the effective observation namespaces. Give each library the same sampling and build eligibility rules.

Keep common random numbers **within** each candidate replicate across cells. The process seed still pairs population/network and outbreak generation. One candidate observation config ID stays constant across cells, with a separate target ID. Effective observation streams are keyed by the latent seed, observation seed and config ID. Reuse each candidate latent run across detection pairs within its own replicate. Independent libraries share the protocol and targets. Each has its own candidate parents, latent runs and observations. [Observation stream derivation](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/src/jersey_outbreak/observation_scheduler.py:61)

After the authorizing ruling is committed, carry forward the builds-only pre-check, deterministic reserve order and frozen parent hashes. Keep and disclose reviewed fallback builds. Reserve exhaustion returns to the owner. Freeze the final ledger before study simulation, with no post-freeze replacement or retry. The draw distribution in §1 is therefore defined over buildable seeds under that declared procedure, including reviewed fallbacks. [G35-B predeclaration, §8](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-10-22-g35b-predeclaration.md:295)

## 4. Per-library gate and across-library reading

Apply **P1–P10 unchanged in meaning** to each library. Keep the frozen grid, objective, summation order, tie and profile-identification rules, tolerances and bias limits. At \(N=20\), each dimension needs **at least 16 hits**, joint recovery needs **at least 12**, and each dimension can have **at most four boundary selections**. Evaluate counts with integer arithmetic and signed errors and bias with exact decimal arithmetic. Compute P8 independently even though P5 implies it on this grid. P10 checks the library’s declared work and the shared target work, which is performed and counted once. The harness hard-codes the historical targets and caps, so the successor needs a separate orchestration and evaluator that reuse the frozen scoring functions. [Frozen gate](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-10-22-g35b-predeclaration.md:216) · [Existing evaluator](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/src/jersey_outbreak/g35b_design_study.py:481)

For a complete, auditable campaign with the shared target predicates satisfied, let \(K\) be the number of library PASS verdicts. Freeze the following study-level reading, using one-sided binomial tails under the independent-draw model conditional on the panel.

| \(K\) out of 22 | Reading | Boundary at \(p=0.80\) |
|---:|---|---|
| 21–22 | Yes, evidence above the reliability standard | \(P(K\ge21)=0.0480\) |
| 0–13 | No, evidence below the reliability standard | \(P(K\le13)=0.0201\) |
| 14–20 | Can’t tell whether reliability exceeds the standard | \(P(K\le14)=0.0561\), so 14 stays unresolved |

Equality at a per-library threshold still meets that predicate. **20/22 stays unresolved.** At **21/22**, the positive reading explicitly reports the one observed flip. Publish all verdicts, predicate counts and signed margins, plus target-by-library joint hits and selected offsets. The same-panel mixed verdicts are direct evidence of draw sensitivity, independently of the reliability reading.

This is an explicit **new study-level rule** under Steven’s B2 decision. The owner rules on the proposed cutoffs and wording before freeze. Each library keeps its own PASS or FAIL. There is no pooled recovery gate, best-library selection or reduced denominator. A shared P2 failure remains a scientific FAIL for each library and yields “can’t tell about library dependence.” Apply the same interpretive rule to a shared P3 failure in an otherwise valid campaign. A software or integrity defect follows the frozen VOID process and yields no numerical reading. [Owner authority for a new study-level rule](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-11-20-g41-owner-ruling.md:74)

## 5. Concurrent R = 3 reference

Score **all four batches in each library** on the same targets. For each batch, report the P4–P8 criteria as “met” or “not met,” with ties, dimension and joint hits, exact mean biases, boundary counts and offset profiles. For each parent library, show the R = 12 joint and offset counts beside its four R = 3 counts, and report how many of its four batches met the criteria. Retain the frozen four-batch offset agreement and fixed-cell spread, and keep the pre-specified nested R = 6 re-profile as descriptive lineage.

The pairing shows whether pooling twelve replicates tends to help relative to their constituent batches, and whether weak offset recovery persists after pooling. The 88 batch evaluations are nested in 22 parent comparisons and use one target panel. A co-primary gate would answer a different question and could make the R = 12 reading depend on how well a short library happened to score. The reference adds scoring and retention work with zero additional builds, latent calls or report transforms. Report spreads as stochastic replicate variation. [Frozen arm-(i) descriptives](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-10-22-g35b-predeclaration.md:237) · [Implemented offset-agreement rule](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/src/jersey_outbreak/g35b_design_study.py:1225)

## 6. What I would change in the framing

I would call this **conditional gate reliability**, and report draw sensitivity alongside it. “Recovery is a property of R = 12” can sound like invariance or a claim about arbitrary fresh targets. A design that must generalize over both axes needs multiple independently drawn target panels crossed with independently drawn libraries, with a separate declaration of how to read the two sources of variation.

I would also distinguish a more stable fit from an explanation of the ridge. At a fixed cell and date, the expected squared distance from a fixed transformed target to a candidate mean includes a candidate-variance term divided by \(R\). If that variance differs across cells, changing \(R\) can change the expected finite-library loss ordering as well as its scatter. The exploratory ridge diagnostic supports examining both fixed-cell losses and re-profiled offsets. It leaves a joint beta–offset–detection tradeoff plausible. [Ridge diagnostic, §§Q3–Q5](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/research/v1_3/2026-09-29-phase0b-ridge-diagnostic-EXPLORATORY.md:41)

Use a fixed design and complete every blind score before any truth join. If Steven’s decision requires target-panel generalization or finer discrimination near 80%, settle that before implementation. A fixed conditional study cannot supply either conclusion just by interpreting its counts more aggressively.

## 7. Compute and work caps

Proposed **exact campaign counts** at \(L=22,\ N=20\), with four R = 3 reference surfaces and one R = 6 descriptive surface per target and library:

| Work | Count |
|---|---:|
| Parent builds \(N+12L\) | 284 |
| 30-day latent calls \(N+300L\) | 6,620 |
| Report transforms \(N+7{,}500L\) | 165,020 |
| Candidate cells across libraries \(625L\) | 13,750 |
| Gate loss evaluations \(625NL\) | 275,000 |
| Descriptive loss evaluations \(5\times625NL\) | 1,375,000 |

Freeze separate shared-target and per-library counts and require reconciliation to the exact totals. The builds-only pre-check is separately capped at 284 plus the owner-approved, frozen reserve count.

**EXPLORATORY planning estimates from retained disclosures.** G35-B arm (i) took 869 seconds for nine new replicates, reusing batch A and the targets. Scaling generation to all twelve and adding the shared targets and scoring puts 22 libraries at **roughly seven hours serial** on comparable hardware. Budget on the order of **a few GiB** for retained evidence. That storage estimate uses the historical arm-(i) and B directories as a rough basis and leaves headroom for twice the target records per library and more target truth evidence. Peak working storage and wall time are unmeasured for this design. Treat them as planning estimates and report measured values from a single source. Steven owns execution authorization. [Campaign disclosures](/home/steven/jos-g35b-campaign-20261022T234254Z/campaign_summary.json) · [Exit-audit observations O1 and O4](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/audits/2026-11-19-g35b-campaign-exit-audit-opus-CONFIRMED.md:187)

## 8. Biggest risk and guard

The largest risk is that **one difficult target panel produces a precise answer to a narrow question**. A shared viability failure can force every verdict, and a few difficult targets can place many libraries at the same threshold. G35-B arm (i) met four inclusive counts exactly and had zero four-batch offset agreement. A verdict count alone would hide whether libraries disagree on many targets or flip because of one or two. [Exit audit, observation O5](/home/steven/jos-g42-sol-ro/docs/audits/2026-11-19-g35b-campaign-exit-audit-opus-CONFIRMED.md:194)

Freeze the conditional wording, the common-predicate rule and the target-by-library diagnostics. Keep the entire panel regardless of viability, fallback status or influence. Retain raw target transmission and observation evidence, candidate tables, seed lineage, blind records and read-back hashes in a sealed bundle. Have the exit audit recompute the recovery and study readings, and state exactly which P3 facts the retained evidence can independently establish. Complete this read-only consult at HEAD `6708ad238860872a5ea548943fc004a0424cb261`.

## Where I expect other designers to differ

Some will prefer fewer libraries and a descriptive count. That can show a same-panel flip at lower cost. My proposal spends more to put an explicit evidence standard behind a positive reliability claim. Others may choose unanimity, a different reliability threshold, or multiple target panels. Those choices reflect different tolerances for library failures or a different population of targets, and should be settled by the owner before sizing the study.
